INT. ABANDONED SALT-LICK CHAPEL - DAY

A tin-roofed chapel on the edge of the alkali flats. Pews pulled out and stacked against the wall. In the middle of the floor, a block of rock salt the size of a coffin, glittering where the light finds it.

HOLLIS BRANDT-OKAFOR stands in the doorway, dust to his knees. He is lean, sunburned, and carries a rifle like it weighs nothing. He lets his eyes adjust.

Across the room, a woman sits on the altar rail with her boots swinging. She does not look up when he enters. She is peeling an orange with a skinning knife.

At the back, in the pulpit, a boy no older than fifteen crouches with a canvas sack in his arms. He is trying to be invisible.

HOLLIS
Mercy Calloway.

MERCY
Marshal Brandt. You're a long way from a courthouse.

HOLLIS
Forty miles. The horse died twenty back.

She cuts a slice of orange and eats it.

MERCY
Then you walked into the one place I know you'd come.

HOLLIS
The boy.

MERCY
He's not mine.

HOLLIS
That's not what the telegraph said.

MERCY
Telegraph says a lot.

HOLLIS
It said you took his father's payroll. It said you took the father, too.

MERCY
It said that.

HOLLIS
Did you?

MERCY
Do you want me to say yes so you can hang me, or no so you can call me a liar?

HOLLIS
I want the truth.

MERCY
Then you're the first man in this territory who does.

She finally looks at the boy. He shrinks.

MERCY (CONT'D)
Tell him, Eli. Tell him what you saw.

ELI
I -- my pa --

HOLLIS
Easy, son.

ELI
My pa shot first.

HOLLIS
At who?

ELI
At her. She was only standing there.

HOLLIS
Standing where?

ELI
By the well. She had the sack, she was giving it back.

MERCY
I told you, boy, that's enough.

HOLLIS
No, let him finish.

ELI
My pa wanted it for himself. He wanted all of it. He said if she lived, she'd talk.

HOLLIS
And the father?

ELI
He fell in the well.

MERCY
He fell. Not pushed. Fell.

HOLLIS
Eli, did she push him?

ELI
No, sir.

HOLLIS
Then why did you run?

ELI
Because everybody said she did.

A long silence. Salt ticks in the heat. Outside, wind starts pulling at the tin roof.

HOLLIS
I walked forty miles for a killer.

MERCY
And now?

HOLLIS
Now I've got a witness and a thief and a sack of money that belongs to a dead man's widow.

MERCY
That sounds like a mess, Marshal.

HOLLIS
It sounds like a trial.

MERCY
You'd put a fifteen-year-old on a stand against his own blood?

HOLLIS
I'd put the truth on a stand. He's just how it gets there.

ELI
I'll do it.

MERCY
Eli --

ELI
I'll do it, Miss Mercy. Somebody ought to say it out loud.

Mercy slides off the altar rail. She folds the knife and sets it on the salt block, handle toward Hollis.

MERCY
Then I suppose I'm in your custody.

HOLLIS
I suppose you are.

She holds out her wrists. He doesn't take them. He lifts the rifle, turns, and opens the chapel door to a white and blinding sky.

HOLLIS (CONT'D)
Walk with me. It's forty miles.

MERCY
And the boy?

HOLLIS
He walks too.

The three of them step out into the glare.
